@misc{sql-F8-diskfull-on-this-store-is-not-disk,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {a psycopg DiskFull from this store is a shared-memory segment placement failure, not disk exhaustion, and its message names the wrong cause},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=sppf-ingestion. Measured LIVE 2026-08-24 — two corpus-scale anti-joins over obs_node and obs failed with 'could not resize shared memory segment to 134217728 bytes: No space left on device', once with CONTEXT parallel worker, while df -h /dev/shm read 7.2G avail at 7 percent used and SHOW dynamic_shared_memory_type returned posix. The backend does not see this /dev/shm. Remedy is to bound parallelism or scope the query, not to free disk. ⚑ The placement claim is INFERRED from df, not directly observed — data_directory is refused with InsufficientPrivilege for this role. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): A psycopg DiskFull raised by this store is NOT disk exhaustion, and its own message names the wrong cause. Measured LIVE 2026-08-24 — two corpus-scale anti-joins over obs_node and obs failed with 'could not resize shared memory segment /PostgreSQL.852091198 to 134217728 bytes: No space left on device', once with CONTEXT: parallel worker. df -h /dev/shm at that moment read 7.7G size, 519M used, 7.2G avail, 7 percent. SHOW dynamic_shared_memory_type is posix. So the 128MiB segment could not be placed despite 7.2G free — the postgres backend does not see this /dev/shm (container, cgroup, or private mount namespace), and the error text attributes to SPACE what is a VISIBILITY or placement failure. ⚑ THE HAZARD IS THAT IT READS AS A CAPACITY INCIDENT — the honest next step is to bound parallelism for the session (SET max_parallel_workers_per_gather = 0 succeeds via schema.py --probe, but each probe is a fresh connection so it does not persist) or to scope the query, NOT to free disk. Confirming the backend's actual /dev/shm needs pg_read_all_settings, which this role lacks — data_directory is refused with InsufficientPrivilege, so the placement claim is INFERRED from the df reading, not directly observed.}
}

@misc{sql-F9-cancel-had-no-mutation-intent,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {schema.py --cancel was a declared store-writer whose branch tested neither --apply nor --dry-run, and its PID operand is a REUSED key, so a stale number could stop a different backend than the one read.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=sppf-ingestion. REPAIRED 2026-08-24 under Ⓢ7 ⟡cancel-mutation-intent. The only silent-default writer of the seven _WRITES_STORE modes, and the only one whose mutation is unrecoverable. --dry-run now RE-READS pg_stat_activity for the PID instead of echoing the operand, which is what closes the reuse window. Verified LIVE 2026-08-24. check_mode_contracts went 6 to 5 and schema --selftest held at 221/221. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): REPAIRED 2026-08-24. schema.py --cancel declared writes=('store',) and its branch at :1055 tested NEITHER --apply NOR --dry-run — the ONLY silent-default writer among the seven _WRITES_STORE modes, and the one whose mutation is UNRECOVERABLE (a cancelled promote is half-finished; --terminate rolls back a transaction). Its operand made it worse than a convention gap: a PID is NOT a stable key, so between the --promotes read that chose it and the write that acts on it the number can be REUSED and denote a different backend — under a live swarm, a sibling's writer. Fixed: refuses when --apply and --dry-run disagree (both is as wrong as neither), and --dry-run RE-READS pg_stat_activity for that PID rather than echoing the operand back, closing the reuse window. Verified LIVE 2026-08-24: bare --cancel 99999999 exits 2 with the refusal; --dry-run on absent PID exits 1 naming the reuse hazard; --dry-run on live pid 149337 printed usename/state/ran_for/query and stopped nothing. check_mode_contracts 6 -> 5 (schema row cleared); schema --selftest 221/221 before and after.}
}

@misc{sql-F10-verify-is-a-census-artifact,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {sppf_db --verify is not an undocumented mode and must not be documented — it does not dispatch, and the census row reporting it is a fact about the reader, which treats a docstring mention as a declaration.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=sppf-ingestion. REFUTES the Ⓢ10 brief, which assigned --verify as one of ten modes to document. Measured 2026-08-24 via pycodemod --literal over scripts/sppf_db.py: ONE site, role=doc, at sppf_db.py:363 inside write_events' docstring. No compare site, no argv branch, no usage line. A comment at sppf_db.py:4129 already records the deliberate removal and states that a usage line for a retired mode is worse than none, because that block IS the tool's self-description. Writing the line to clear the census row would make the tool assert a capability it does not have. Same shape as the --calls emit census that missed _emit_one — the instrument's boundary reported as the subject's. Left undocumented deliberately, with the reason written at the site. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): REFUTES the brief. sppf_db --verify was assigned to me as one of ten modes to DOCUMENT. It must NOT be documented: it does not dispatch. Measured 2026-08-24 with pycodemod --literal -- '--verify' scripts/sppf_db.py: ONE site, role=doc, at sppf_db.py:363, inside write_events' docstring. No compare site, no argv branch, no usage line. toolmodes --divergence nevertheless reports it UNBACKED documented, no matching flag literal, because its reader treats a docstring mention as a declaration. A comment at sppf_db.py:4129 already records the deliberate removal and says a usage line for a retired mode is worse than none, since the block IS the tool's self-description. So the census row is a fact about the READER, not the tool, and clearing it by writing the line would make the tool assert a capability it does not have. Same shape as the --calls emit census that missed _emit_one: the instrument's boundary reported as the subject's. Left UNDOCUMENTED deliberately, with the reason written at the site.}
}

@misc{sql-F11-declared-and-printed-are-two-spellings,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A mode roster has two censuses reading two different spellings — check_mode_contracts imports MODES at runtime, toolmodes reads the usage lines from source — so a post-hoc roster row satisfies one and is invisible to the other, and a mode is documented only when its line is readable FROM SOURCE.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=sppf-ingestion. Measured 2026-08-24 on jea/metalanguage/schema.py. --establish, --migrate-views and --transport were correct paydowns of the roster half that left the usage half untouched, and read UNDOCUMENTED for as long as they existed. My first repair made the PRINTER read MODES: bare output became correct, the census moved by ZERO, and --emit and --reap newly joined the undocumented list. The fix belonged in the source-visible _MODES pairs. Corollary surfaced by the same change: three pairs entries begin --coverage and all split_flag to one key, so last-wins had silently made MODES['--coverage'].why read as --reap's destructive sentence, unobservable while the printer read _MODES. Result: schema.py left the divergence census entirely and check_mode_contracts reached 0. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Two censuses read a mode roster by DIFFERENT spellings, so paying one down does not pay the other, and a fix aimed at the wrong one moves nothing. check_mode_contracts IMPORTS the module and reads the runtime MODES object, so a post-hoc MODES[flag] = Mode(...) row satisfies it. toolmodes --divergence reads the file's SOURCE for the usage lines a bare run would print, so the same row is invisible to it however the bare run actually renders. Measured 2026-08-24: schema.py --establish, --migrate-views and --transport were each added as post-hoc rows when the contract gate caught them dispatching in no roster — a CORRECT paydown of the roster half that left the usage half untouched, and all three then read UNDOCUMENTED for as long as they existed. My first repair made the PRINTER read MODES. Bare output became correct and the census moved by ZERO, with --emit and --reap newly joining the undocumented list. The fix had to go into the source-visible _MODES pairs. A mode is documented when its line is READABLE FROM SOURCE, not when the tool can be made to print it. census-keyed-on-one-spelling, with the two spellings being DECLARED and PRINTED. Corollary found by the same change: three _MODES entries begin --coverage, all three split_flag to the key --coverage, so last-wins had silently made MODES['--coverage'].why read as --reap's sentence — the roster described the census mode as 'delete ORPHAN keys only'. Unobservable while the printer read _MODES, because the pairs still rendered correctly and nothing read the corrupted roster entry.}
}

@misc{sql-F12-sub-level-slice-is-unreached,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Ⓢ5 sub-level slicing is UNREACHED rather than refuted, because the store indexes the per-core question and not the which-cores-are-biggest question, so the SIZING step is the expensive half.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=sppf-ingestion. Measured LIVE 2026-08-24 with schema.py --plan, executing nothing. Grouping obs by core_id corpus-wide plans at cost 3,885,430 over a Parallel Seq Scan of 23.5M rows with 6 extra workers. A single-core count plans at 28,787 as an Index Only Scan on ix_obs_pk, estimated 41,294 rows — 135x cheaper. --spread ran past 17 minutes without returning. This is meta-F1 in my OWN work: reporting 'a sub-level slice is unnecessary' would have been a claim about the STORE when the honest statement is that the ranking query is unaffordable under load and I never saw the seven units. Unbuilt cheap path: obs.local_id is dense per core per --fibre, so max(local_id) per core is an index-supported size proxy needing no 23.5M-row aggregate. Context: 28 IO-bound backends on the tenant, oldest 54 minutes, lock-waiting 0 throughout — saturation, not a hang, which matters because --locks reads healthy. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Ⓢ5 sub-level slicing is NOT REFUTED and NOT BUILT — it is UNREACHED, and the blocker is the SIZING query, not the slice. To size the 7 largest ingestion units I must group obs by core_id corpus-wide. Measured LIVE 2026-08-24 with schema.py --plan, which executes nothing: SELECT core_id, count(*) FROM obs GROUP BY core_id ORDER BY n DESC LIMIT 7 plans at cost 3,885,430 over a Parallel Seq Scan of 23.5M rows with 6 additional workers planned. The same file's --spread ran past 17 minutes without returning and was still in flight when I stopped. By contrast SELECT count(*) FROM obs WHERE core_id = 'x' plans at cost 28,787 as an Index Only Scan on ix_obs_pk, estimated 41,294 rows — 135x cheaper. So the store INDEXES the per-core question and does NOT index the which-cores-are-biggest question, and the sizing step is the expensive half of Ⓢ5. This is meta-F1 in my own work, not the dispatcher's: I would have reported 'a sub-level slice is unnecessary' as a finding about the STORE when the honest statement is that the ranking query is unaffordable under current load and I never saw the seven units. A cheap sizing path exists and is unbuilt: obs.local_id is dense per core per --fibre, so max(local_id) per core is an index-supported proxy for core size that needs no aggregate over 23.5M rows. Also load-relevant: 28 backends were IO-bound on this tenant throughout, oldest at 54 minutes, five parallel workers on a single COUNT(*) FROM obs, and a DELETE FROM obs WHERE core_id=$1 cycling. Lock-waiting was 0 the whole time, so this is throughput saturation and not a hang — an important distinction because --locks reads healthy.}
}

