@misc{sql-F13-every-level-is-already-slice-safe,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A slice WITHIN a packing level is already correct by construction and needs no new machinery, because both height builders predicate only on a row's own children against the committed map and never read a row emitted in the same round.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=sppf-ingestion. ANSWERS Ⓢ5 and refutes its implicit premise that sub-level slicing needs new machinery. Read from the owning builders 2026-08-24: q_ht_seed selects leaves by NOT EXISTS(edge WHERE plid = local_id). q_ht_step emits a parent only when no child is missing from _ht_map, evaluated against rows committed by PRIOR rounds. So partitioning a round into N statements, each with an added plid range predicate, yields the identical map. q_ht_step's docstring already states the invariant, from sppf_tree.run_rounds: each statement commits and the next resolves children by PK lookup against a fresh snapshot. WHY IT IS NEEDED, measured LIVE 2026-08-24 on bbRKTCildS8, 4,592,031 obs over 73 rounds: ht 0 holds 2,597,146 rows at 56.6 percent, then 712,725 / 616,586 / 329,003 / 147,422, and ht 5 to 72 hold about 189,000 rows across 68 rounds with the last 16 rounds carrying one row each. A per-level commit moves the peak to 2,597,146, a 1.77x reduction that is the CEILING because ht 0 alone bounds it. The schedule is doubly lopsided: 5 rounds carry 95.9 percent of the work, 68 rounds carry 4.1 percent. Slicing ht 0 is the highest-value change and needs only a range predicate. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Ⓢ5 ANSWERED, and the brief's implicit premise that a sub-level slice needs NEW MACHINERY is REFUTED. A slice WITHIN a level is already correct by construction — no new builder, no ordering guarantee, no machinery. Read from the two owning builders 2026-08-24: q_ht_seed selects leaves by NOT EXISTS(edge WHERE plid = local_id), a predicate on that row's OWN children only. q_ht_step emits a parent only when NO child of it is missing from _ht_map, again a predicate on that row's own children, evaluated against _ht_map as COMMITTED BY PRIOR ROUNDS. Neither builder reads a row emitted in the SAME round. Therefore partitioning one round into N statements — each with an added range predicate on plid — yields the IDENTICAL map, because readiness never depends on the current round's own output. q_ht_step's own docstring already states the invariant it rests on, from sppf_tree.run_rounds: each statement COMMITS and the next resolves children by an ordinary PK lookup against a FRESH snapshot. A sub-level slice is that same property applied one level finer. WHY IT IS NEEDED, measured LIVE 2026-08-24 on bbRKTCildS8, the largest core, 4,592,031 obs over 73 rounds: ht 0 holds 2,597,146 rows at 56.6 percent, then 712,725 / 616,586 / 329,003 / 147,422, and ht 5 through 72 hold about 189,000 rows across 68 rounds with the last 16 rounds carrying exactly ONE row each. So a per-LEVEL commit moves the peak 4,592,031 to 2,597,146 — 1.77x, and that is the CEILING, bounded by ht 0 alone. Confirms the brief exactly. The schedule is doubly lopsided: 5 rounds carry 95.9 percent of the work and 68 rounds carry 4.1 percent, so the same schedule that under-bounds the peak also over-issues statements at the tail. Slicing ht 0 into 8 is the single highest-value change and needs only a range predicate.}
}

@misc{sql-F14-slice-ht0-already-shipped,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {Ⓢ5 sub-level slicing is BUILT and DEFAULT-ON at sppf_db.py:637-693 (SLICE_ROWS=25000, _pack_level_sliced, pack_core_sql slice_rows=SLICE_ROWS), so the brief's BUILD IT was a non-command — what was genuinely missing is the COMMIT COUNT, the only observable that separates a sliced level from a one-window one.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=sppf-ingestion-r3. Witness — read the compiled q_lvl_emit (local_id range predicate) and q_lvl_assign, read pack_core_sql's default arg, then --equiv pack.sql pack.python IDENTICAL on wpk3blnvCNA (226 classes over 176,818 obs) and qfL4hdKTgHs (523 classes over 683,839 obs), live postgres 2026-08-24. --equiv could not have failed either way, which is why the commit count was built. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Ⓢ5 ⟡slice-ht0 WAS ALREADY BUILT AND DEFAULT-ON, and the brief's 'BUILD IT' is meta-F1's fifth named shape — a NON-COMMAND. Found at scripts/sppf_db.py:637-693: SLICE_ROWS = 25_000 and _pack_level_sliced, reached from pack_core_sql(:939) whose signature is slice_rows=SLICE_ROWS, i.e. ON BY DEFAULT for every caller. It is exactly the design the brief specified: a local_id RANGE predicate (q_lvl_emit: WHERE a.local_id >= lo AND a.local_id < hi) over a level assigned WHOLE into a temp _lvl_assign, so the dense_rank() ranking stays indivisible while the WRITE is windowed, one transaction per window, with ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING making a partly-emitted level re-runnable. The docstring already records why the ranking cannot be sliced: a dense_rank() over a positional slice mints different ids for the same signature, measured on wpk3blnvCNA ht 0 at 176,818 observations to 226 signatures scattered across every local_id. SO THE ASYMMETRY meta-F1 PREDICTS HELD AGAIN: had I taken 'BUILD IT' at face value I would have added a second slicing path beside a working one, in the file whose arc has removed the materialise-then-discard shape three times. THE RESIDUE IS REAL BUT IT IS NOT THE BUILD: the commit count -- which _pack_level_sliced's OWN docstring names as the only evidence the slicing works, because --equiv compares the PARTITION and is IDENTICAL either way BY CONSTRUCTION -- was computed and discarded, so nothing could distinguish a genuinely-sliced level from a one-window one. That is what round 3 built.}
}

@misc{sql-F15-equiv-rowcount-is-not-slice-grain,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {An --equiv row count is the SIGNATURE-CLASS count, not the observation count, so it cannot say whether a level exercised the slicing — sppf_db --pack-level now reports the commit grain (windows over the local_id SPAN, which is sparse, not rows/SLICE_ROWS) and labels a one-window level as a verdict that could not have failed.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=sppf-ingestion-r3. Witness — live postgres 2026-08-24. wpk3blnvCNA ht 0 = 176,818 obs over span 313,828 = 13 windows (rows/25000 would predict 8). qfL4hdKTgHs ht 0 = 683,839 obs over span 1,217,541 = 49 windows, which I had read as 'one window' from its 523-class --equiv output. Both GENUINELY SLICED, so the Ⓢ5 equivalence stands on cores that could have failed. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): ⟡slice-ht0-witness — THE --equiv ROW COUNT IS THE PARTITION SIZE AND SAYS NOTHING ABOUT SLICE GRAIN, AND I MISREAD IT MYSELF WITHIN ONE TURN OF BUILDING THE MODE THAT FIXES IT. --equiv pack.sql pack.python on qfL4hdKTgHs printed '523 row(s) IDENTICAL' and I wrote that the core was 'far under 25,000, so slicing is one window'. FALSE. pack.sql emits ONE ROW PER node_id whose value is the array_agg of its member local_ids, so 523 is the number of SIGNATURE CLASSES. The core actually holds 683,839 observations at ht 0 and commits in 49 windows. The brief WARNED about exactly this ('a PASS on a core whose ht 0 is one slice proves nothing, so pick one that genuinely slices and say which') and the warning did not save me, because the instrument reported a number that LOOKED like a size and was a size of something else. THE FIX IS THE MODE, NOT THE CAUTION: sppf_db --pack-level now prints the commit grain beside the collapse ratio, and labels a one-window level '⚑ ONE WINDOW — an equivalence PASS here does NOT exercise the slicing'. Measured live postgres 2026-08-24: wpk3blnvCNA ht 0 = 176,818 obs, span 313,828, 13 windows. qfL4hdKTgHs ht 0 = 683,839 obs, span 1,217,541, 49 windows. BOTH GENUINELY SLICED, so the Ⓢ5 equivalence stands on cores that could have failed. ⚑ AND THE GRAIN IS A SPAN, NOT A ROW COUNT: local_id is the shim's print counter over the WHOLE core, so a level's ids are SPARSE within their range — 176,818 rows take 13 windows where rows/SLICE_ROWS predicts 8. Reporting the ratio would have understated the grain by 40 percent. THIS IS THE SAME SHAPE AS --pack-level's OWN 'BLIND ON' BLOCK, one layer out: that block refuses a signature-key verdict over a level where a component takes one value, and this refuses a slicing verdict over a level that takes one window.}
}

@misc{sql-F16-fanin-head-is-not-categorical,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The fan-in head cut is neither categorical nor missing — the same head text (Name) is scaffolding at one node_id and content at another, genuine content (Substrate.Foundation.Eq.≡, fanin 1,639) sits inside the head band, and the threshold arm already exists as shared_subtree's baked fanin>=2 plus q_shared_subtree's min_fanin bindparam, which reproduces the brief's 73-child band exactly.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=sppf-ingestion-r3. Witness — live postgres 2026-08-24. query_defs --rows fanin --param lim=100 shows node_id 9245 head Substrate.Foundation.Eq.≡ fanin 1,639 at rank 46, and head text Name recurring at ranks 6/14/15/22/32/42 across fanins 14,328 down to 656. --rows shared_subtree --param min_fanin=1000 returns exactly 73 rows. Corpus figures reconfirmed unmoved, so ⟡transcript-indexer's ARTEFACT_DF_FRAC and ARTEFACT_DF_MIN need no change. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Ⓢ6 ⟡fanin-head-cut — THE BRIEF OFFERED TWO FRAMINGS AND THE DATA REFUTES BOTH, so the honest answer is that the cut belongs in NO layer as a default. Framing 1, the brief's own: 'a THRESHOLD IS THE WRONG INSTRUMENT — the split is categorical (AST scaffolding vs content), and a percentile moves as the corpus grows.' Framing 2, implied by asking builder-vs-view-vs-mode: that some layer should carry it. MEASURED live postgres 2026-08-24 via query_defs --rows fanin --param lim=100. (a) THE HEAD IS NOT CATEGORICALLY SCAFFOLDING. At rank 46 sits node_id 9245, head Substrate.Foundation.Eq.≡, fanin 1,639 — genuine repo content wedged between ArgInfo at 1,746 and #Int:4 at 1,590. A cut that removes the head removes this. (b) A HEAD-TEXT BLOCKLIST CANNOT WORK EITHER, which is what 'categorical' would have to mean operationally. The head text 'Name' recurs at ranks 6, 14, 15, 22, 32, 42 and on down, at fanins from 14,328 to 656 — the SAME head text is scaffolding at one node_id and content at another, because a Name node interns a particular name. The discriminator is the node_id, and node_id is exactly what a category cannot name. (c) AND THE THRESHOLD ARM ALREADY EXISTS AND ALREADY LANDS ON THE BRIEF'S OWN BAND. shared_subtree is a subquery (query_decl.py:279, body shared_subtree_sel in schema_decl) with fanin >= 2 BAKED IN — the low-tail cut, the 58.2 percent at fanin 1, already gone at the view — and q_shared_subtree(query_decl.py:2871) adds a min_fanin bindparam on top. --rows shared_subtree --param min_fanin=1000 returns EXACTLY 73 ROWS, the brief's '73 children absorb 27.1 percent of edges' reproduced by a parameter that has been there all along. SO: the tail cut is a VIEW (correct — fanin 1 carries no sharing signal by definition, a structural fact that cannot drift), and the head cut is a CALLER'S PARAMETER (correct — it is a purpose-relative choice, and 9245 proves no default is right for every purpose). Building a default head cut would bake one consumer's purpose into a shared relation. ⚑ THE FAN-IN NUMBERS DID NOT MOVE — 440,604 distinct children, 1,733,430 edges, max 87,359, and the 73-row band all reconfirmed — so ⟡transcript-indexer's ARTEFACT_DF_FRAC / ARTEFACT_DF_MIN need no change.}
}

@misc{sql-F17-unknown-param-was-a-noop,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {query_defs --param silently dropped an unrecognised key, so --rows node_head --param lim=5 returned the whole 23.3 MB corpus as an answer to a capped question — now refuses with exit 2 and names the binds the builder accepts (query_defs.py:624-655).},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=sppf-ingestion-r3. Witness — live postgres 2026-08-24. q_node_head compiles with no lim bind while sibling q_fanin ends LIMIT %(lim)s, so the flag is load-bearing on one builder and inert on the next. _override_params already handled both --param SPELLINGS on the stated ground that silent ignoring reports the empty case as an answer, and stopped one step short of the unknown KEY. After the fix, --rows node_head --param lim=5 exits 2 and --rows fanin --param lim=3 returns 3 rows. Selftests query_defs 19/19, sppf_db 243/243, schema 221/221. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): ⟡param-refuses — query_defs --param SILENTLY DROPPED AN UNRECOGNISED KEY, so a capped query returned the uncapped corpus in the shape of an answer to the capped question. MEASURED live postgres 2026-08-24: 'query_defs --rows node_head --param lim=5' printed 23.3 MB — every node in the corpus. q_node_head declares no lim bindparam, _override_params' inner suffix-match loop found no target, and the function returned the defaults with NO signal. WHAT MAKES IT PLAUSIBLE RATHER THAN OBVIOUS is that the SIBLING builder q_fanin DOES take lim (its compiled text ends 'LIMIT %(lim)s'), so the same flag is load-bearing on one builder and inert on the next, and a reader who just used it successfully has every reason to expect it to work. THE FUNCTION'S OWN DOCSTRING ALREADY ARGUED THE CASE AND STOPPED ONE STEP SHORT: it handles BOTH --param spellings deliberately, on the stated ground that 'Silently ignoring one spelling is how an instrument reports the empty case as an answer.' An unknown KEY is that same defect one level in — the spelling parsed, the key matched nothing. Its ⚑⚑ block records the twin incident: '--scans pack_level --sandbox printed 0 loop-adjusted rows against a store holding 184,278 obs. Nothing was wrong -- the question was never asked.' FIXED at query_defs.py:624-655: an unmatched key now REFUSES with exit 2 and NAMES the binds the builder accepts, stripping the compiler's _N numbering. Naming the roster is half the fix — 'unknown param' alone sends the reader to --sql to hand-parse placeholders out of compiled text, which is the read-a-compiled-artifact-by-hand shape this module refuses everywhere else. Selftests after: query_defs 19/19, sppf_db 243/243, schema 221/221 — all unchanged from baseline.}
}

