@misc{sql-F22-tenant-mandatory-landed,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {An unstated tenant REFUSES by default, SUBSTRATE_EXPLICIT_TENANT is no longer read, and require= is the only opt-out because it names a tenant rather than skipping one.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=sppf-ingestion-r5. Ⓢ19 ⟡tenant-mandatory. store.tenant exits 2 unconditionally on 'unstated' and the env var is removed from store.py. Gates after -- schema 224/224 both arms, sppf_db 243/243 both arms, query_defs 19/19. The advisory's stale '~20 dispatch sites' justification was the only thing holding the flip. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Ⓢ19 ⟡tenant-mandatory LANDED 2026-08-24. store.tenant now sys.exit(2) UNCONDITIONALLY on an unstated tenant; SUBSTRATE_EXPLICIT_TENANT is no longer consulted in store.py at all -- its role did not invert, it ENDED, because any env-readable route back to 'default to LIVE' is the silent decay the operator's ruling forbids. THE ONE SURVIVING OPT-OUT IS require=, and it is NOT a bypass: it STATES a tenant in code at a named, typed site that refuses an unrecognised value; it does not leave the tenant unnamed. There is no fourth arm of _tenant_named, so an unnamed tenant is unreachable by construction. The advisory's log_fallthrough census is KEPT (the refusal tells THIS invocation what it did; the stream says WHICH SITES keep doing it -- deleting it would trade a measured paydown for a per-run error message). Gates: schema 221->224/224 both arms, sppf_db 243/243 both arms, query_defs 19/19. THE BRIEF'S 'let it break and report where' PRODUCED EXACTLY ONE REAL BREAKAGE, not the ~20 the stale docstrings predicted: the '~20 dispatch sites do not pass a flag today' clause in BOTH store.tenant and ratchet.store_argv was STALE -- the population had already been paid down and the advisory was being kept alive by its own obsolete justification. A ratchet whose census is quoted from a docstring instead of re-derived is the CLAUDE.md memory-cap defect in policy form.}
}

@misc{sql-F23-unsayable-tenant-is-the-bypass,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A tool that declares --sandbox but not --live has an UNSAYABLE tenant, which is a bypass no tenant census can see because it never calls tenant().},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=sppf-ingestion-r5. query_defs.py --selftest --live exited 2 on argparse. Its private _cost_connect(sandbox) held the inlined 'else connect()' default. Invisible to --calls tenant, to --guarded sandbox_connect (reports 'top'), and to --literal SUBSTRATE_EXPLICIT_TENANT. The discriminator is WHO CHOOSES, not where the call sits. Repaired via store.tenant(require=...) plus a declared --live. 19/19 before and after. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): THE FLIP LOCATED EXACTLY ONE REAL BREAKAGE AND IT IS A CLASS THE TENANT CENSUS COULD NOT SEE. query_defs.py --selftest --live exited 2 on argparse 'unrecognized arguments: --live' -- the tool declared --sandbox and NEVER --live, so its tenant was UNSAYABLE. Its private _cost_connect(sandbox) read 'sandbox_connect(reset=False) if sandbox else connect(mode=ro)', the exact inlined shape schema_rows._cli_connect exists to absorb, wearing a helper's name so it did not read as one. ⚑⚑ IT WAS INVISIBLE TO EVERY EXISTING PROBE: pycodemod --calls tenant misses it (it never calls tenant), --guarded sandbox_connect reports it 'top' i.e. the library-parameter shape that _cli_connect's note says MUST NOT be consolidated, and --literal SUBSTRATE_EXPLICIT_TENANT finds nothing. THE DISCRIMINATOR IS WHO CHOOSES, NOT WHERE THE CALL SITS: all six _cost_connect callers are in-file and all six thread a.sandbox down from main, so it is a dispatch site by provenance while being top-level by position. A shape-based census reports its own boundary as the subject's (the-fact-may-be-an-edge-not-a-property). REPAIRED: _cost_connect routes through store.tenant(require='sandbox' if sandbox else None), and --live is declared in argparse SO THE RESOLVER CAN SEE IT -- deliberately not read at the dispatch, since store.tenant resolves from sys.argv. ⚑ AND THIS IS THE 'MISSING HALF' store.tenant's OWN DOCSTRING DESCRIBES, still live at a dispatch site: 'an option that does not exist cannot be stated, so no-flag MEANT live and nobody had to decide.' The docstring named the defect in the abstract while an instance of it sat two modules away. query_defs 19/19 before and after, --live now accepted.}
}

@misc{sql-F24-a-refusal-case-cannot-take-the-refusal,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A case that exercises a refusal by TAKING it cannot coexist with the refusal being real, so its coverage must be re-aimed at the pure decision rather than given a quieter argv.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=sppf-ingestion-r5. The two schema_selftest cases asserting flagless-routes-LIVE were RETIRED, not migrated -- their subject was deleted by ⟡tenant-mandatory. Coverage relocated onto _tenant_named, which was already split out for exactly this. Three cases replace two -- flagless READ and flagless WRITE both resolve 'unstated', plus a SystemExit case pinning exit 2 because a computed-then-ignored verdict is the pre-2026-08-18 behaviour. schema 221 to 224/224 both arms. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): WHAT THE TWO DELIBERATE CASES BECAME, AND THE ANSWER IS THAT THEIR SUBJECT WAS DELETED RATHER THAN THEIR MECHANISM REPLACED. schema_selftest asserted 'without the flag a READ mode routes live' and a flagless --advance write, both driving _cli_connect through the real resolver. Under ⟡tenant-mandatory there IS no live-by-default branch to assert, so the cases were not migrated -- they were retired and their coverage RELOCATED. ⚑⚑ THE OLD CASE'S OWN NOTE PREDICTED THE FAILURE IT COULD NOT SURVIVE. It argued at length against passing an explicit argv=[...] because that 'would quiet the suite AND STOP EXERCISING ... the advisory-vs-refusal path'. Sound while the refusal was optional. Once the refusal is real the same case ABORTS THE SUITE MID-RUN (sql-F19). A CASE THAT EXERCISES A REFUSAL BY TAKING IT CANNOT COEXIST WITH THE REFUSAL BEING REAL -- the fix is never a quieter argv, it is to stop calling the function that exits. ⚑ THE COVERAGE MOVED DOWN A LAYER RATHER THAN VANISHING, and _tenant_named was ALREADY SPLIT OUT FOR EXACTLY THIS ('the decision alone -- pure, total, and the thing the cases actually pin'). Three cases now stand where two did: two assert a flagless READ and a flagless WRITE dispatch resolve 'unstated' (so a future mode re-inlining 'if --sandbox in sys.argv' still fails here, which was the original case's real purpose), and one catches SystemExit from _cli_connect and pins exit code 2 -- because 'unstated' being COMPUTED and then ignored is precisely the pre-2026-08-18 behaviour, so the verdict alone is not enough. schema 221 -> 224/224 on both arms. ⚑ THE STRUCTURE IS GENERAL: when a policy flip deletes a branch, its tests do not need a different MECHANISM, they need to be re-aimed at the pure decision the branch consumed. The exiting wrapper is untestable by construction from inside its own suite.}
}

@misc{sql-F25-ddl-read-mode-was-already-ruled,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A read mode may NOT run DDL, and that ruling is already implemented at all 19 _tiers sites -- 19 unconditional calls to an ASSERTING function is what full compliance looks like, not unguarded DDL.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=sppf-ingestion-r5. require_tiers IS the ruling in executable form. Both _tiers bodies delegate to it (sppf_drain.py:82, sppf_tree.py:81), one line each, replacing two _schema.apply calls plus an unconditional commit. The guard-shape census cannot distinguish 'unconditional write' from 'unconditional assert', so '0 of 19 under an if' reads as alarming while proving the paydown. Lock hazard measured three times. Nothing blocks the sppf tools on DDL grounds -- see sql-F21 and gate-F15. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Ⓢ18 ⟡ddl-read-mode NEEDS NO RULING BECAUSE THE RULING IS ALREADY IN THE TREE, IMPLEMENTED, at all 19 sites. The brief says 'require_tiers already exists and asserts. What remains is the ruling.' -- but require_tiers IS the ruling in executable form, and its docstring states it as policy: '⟡ddl-is-a-write -- ASSERT every named tier is ESTABLISHED. REFUSE naming --establish. Creates nothing. Commits nothing.' MEASURED, not preferred: CREATE ... IF NOT EXISTS takes the relation lock BEFORE it can discover the object exists, observed three times in this tree (13 backends on ix_terms_text, five workers plus deadlocked pairs on ix_obsnode_pk), and --tree-concurrency/--drain-concurrency fan N processes at these very modes, so the read path was contributing DDL contention to the concurrency measurement it exists to take. VERIFIED LIVE: pycodemod --guarded _tiers gives 19 of 19 sites (10 sppf_drain, 9 sppf_tree), and BOTH _tiers bodies now delegate to _schema.require_tiers -- sppf_drain.py:82 and sppf_tree.py:81, one line each, where they previously ran _schema.apply twice plus an unconditional con.commit(). ⚑ SO THE ANSWER TO 'may a read mode run DDL' IS NO, AND IT IS ALREADY ENFORCED -- the precondition is met ONCE at build time via 'schema.py --establish tree valstage --live --apply', which is where a precondition belongs. ⚑⚑ THE BRIEF READ A LIVE POLICY AS AN OPEN QUESTION, and the tell is the same one meta-F1 names: '0 of 19 under an if' was read as 'unguarded DDL' when it is the OPPOSITE -- 19 unconditional calls to an ASSERTING function is exactly what full compliance looks like. THE GUARD-SHAPE CENSUS CANNOT DISTINGUISH 'unconditional write' FROM 'unconditional assert', so the number that looked alarming is the number that proves the paydown. Nothing blocks sppf_tree/sppf_drain from a selftest declaration on DDL grounds -- what blocks them is sql-F21 (they have no --selftest at all) and gate-F15 (they need a FIXTURE, not a suite).}
}

