@misc{sql-F30-explain-oracle-is-a-parser-not-a-tenant,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The EXPLAIN grammar oracle needed no live store, because live/rw, live/ro and sandbox return identical verdicts on all six dialect cases, so _pg_probe_con is now the sandbox handle and gate-F29 is closed.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=live-store-in-precommit. The witness. _pycodemod_selftest.py:1836 reached store.connect(mode=rw) unconditionally from the pre-commit selftest roster. Three arms by six cases (SELECT, DELETE-alias, age-xmin, RETURNING, IS DISTINCT FROM, prose) identical across live/rw, live/ro and sandbox. The fixture table evq exists in neither store, and UndefinedTable already counts as PARSED, so the live schema was never wanted. Positive control gave current_database=substrate_sandbox. selftest 392/392, roundtrip 4/4. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): gate-F29 CLOSED, and the repair was NOT to declare the tenant but to REMOVE THE CHOICE. _pg_probe_con opened store.connect(mode='rw', back='postgres') -- a LIVE READ-WRITE connection to the main store -- reached unconditionally from _pycodemod_selftest.py:1836, which the pre-commit roster runs. It never called store.tenant, so it never consulted argv: unsayable, not merely undeclared, and a 'selftest-tenant: sandbox' pragma there would have been a FALSE declaration under gate-F24. THE DECIDING OBSERVATION IS THAT THE ORACLE IS POSTGRES' PARSER, AND A PARSER IS NOT A TENANT. The probe's only use is EXPLAIN <stmt> inside con.transaction(), asked of SQL TEXT; the case at :1840 asserts that 'DELETE FROM evq e' is valid postgres and not prose, over a fixture table in a mkdtemp file that exists in NEITHER store -- and the recogniser already counts UndefinedTable / 'does not exist' as PARSED, so it never wanted the live SCHEMA either. WITNESS (scratchpad/probe_rw_witness.py, three arms x six dialect cases): live/rw, live/ro and sandbox returned IDENTICAL verdicts on all six (plain SELECT, DELETE-alias, age(xmin), RETURNING, IS DISTINCT FROM, and the prose case correctly refused as SyntaxError). The live store contributed NOTHING the sandbox did not, because both run the same parser. REPAIR: _pg_probe_con now returns _pg_sandbox_con() -- the throwaway-tenant handle that already existed in the same file for DDL. POSITIVE CONTROL: the probe now reports current_database()='substrate_sandbox' (live is 'substrate'), and _pg_probe_con() is _pg_sandbox_con() is True. pycodemod --selftest 392/392 with the dialect case RUNNING not skipping; --roundtrip 4/4 lossless.}
}

@misc{sql-F31-mode-rw-was-a-copypaste-that-granted-writes,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {mode=rw on the EXPLAIN probe was a copy-paste, not a write intent, and it granted a pre-commit selftest real write access to the live store.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=live-store-in-precommit. The witness. On the live/rw handle CREATE TABLE _witness_rw_probe succeeded. On live/ro the identical statement was refused with ReadOnlySqlTransaction while all six grammar cases stayed identical, so the rw grant was load-bearing for nothing and exercised as a real capability. The _pg_sandbox_con docstring asserted the opposite in as many words, that the EXPLAIN probe cannot mutate, which is true of EXPLAIN and false of the connection. Superseded by the sandbox routing, so neither mode now reaches live. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): THE SHARPEST FACT: mode='rw' on the EXPLAIN probe was a COPY-PASTE, NOT A WRITE INTENT -- and it had been granting write access to a pre-commit selftest. The docstring two functions above it (_pg_sandbox_con) asserted the opposite in as many words: 'SEPARATE FROM _pg_probe_con ON PURPOSE. That one runs EXPLAIN, which cannot mutate.' TRUE OF EXPLAIN, FALSE OF THE CONNECTION -- and the comment written to justify the separation is what made the hazard read as already-considered. WITNESS, run against the LIVE tenant 2026-08-24: on the live/rw handle 'CREATE TABLE _witness_rw_probe (x int)' SUCCEEDED. On live/ro the identical statement was refused, ReadOnlySqlTransaction 'cannot execute CREATE TABLE in a read-only transaction', while all six grammar cases returned identical verdicts. So the rw grant was (a) load-bearing for nothing and (b) a real, exercised write capability, one code path from a census. NOTE the asymmetry with store.connect's own docstring, which records that mode='ro' is NOT ENFORCEABLE under postgres because the tenant role owns its database -- that is true of DML against owned tables, but the measurement shows SET TRANSACTION READ ONLY does refuse DDL, so 'ro' is weaker than sqlite's old guarantee yet strictly stronger than 'rw'. Superseded regardless: the probe is now sandbox-only, so neither mode reaches the live store.}
}

@misc{sql-F32-runtime-tenant-lens-built,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {A runtime tenant lens at all three store openers is decorrelated from the two source censuses by construction, because it observes opens rather than inferring them, and it detects the bypass signature they cannot see.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=live-store-in-precommit. The witness. _lens_record is called from tenant, connect and sandbox_connect, the complete opener roster, so the lens is total over store.py rather than sampled. Four arms measured. An empty list before any reach is the honest zero. tenant(require=sandbox) produced the resolution row plus the delegate row. A direct connect produced a connect/live row with no preceding tenant row, which is the bypass signature and precisely the old _pg_probe_con shape. The real probe recorded sandbox_connect/sandbox from _pycodemod_sql.py:860 where it would previously have recorded connect/live/rw. Recording is unconditional per gate-F24 with only the export sink env-gated, fires before the socket so failed opens are observed, cannot change a verdict, and opens no connection of its own. Not yet consumed by any gate, and per-process by construction. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Ⓐ2 ⟡runtime-tenant-lens BUILT in jea/metalanguage/store.py, and it CLOSES gate-F30's blocker rather than minting the baseline gate-F30 refused. WHY A RUNTIME LENS: the two existing censuses (import-reach and call-site) are CORRELATED -- both ask which code MENTIONS a store API, so both share sql-F23's blind spot, a reacher whose tenant is unsayable because it never calls tenant. Their agreement was never coverage. _pg_probe_con was invisible to --calls tenant for exactly that reason and took seven careful reads to find. SHAPE: one recorder, _lens_record, called from ALL THREE openers -- tenant (store.py:304), connect (:478), sandbox_connect (:519) -- which is the complete roster, so the lens is TOTAL over this module rather than sampled. It records (opener, tenant, mode, label, caller-frame) into an in-process list; lens_report() reads it. FOUR DESIGN CONSTRAINTS, each measured or ruled: (1) NO OPT-OUT FROM BEING OBSERVED per gate-F24 -- recording is unconditional; only the EXPORT SINK is env-gated (SUBSTRATE_TENANT_LENS), and a sink is not the observation. (2) It fires BEFORE the socket, so an open that FAILS is still observed. (3) It cannot change a verdict -- best-effort, swallows everything, returns None, same contract as ratchet.log_fallthrough, because these paths run inside pre-commit gates. (4) It opens no connection of its own, so it never becomes a fourth reacher measuring itself. WITNESS (scratchpad/verify_lens.py, four arms): arm 0 empty list = the honest zero; arm 1 tenant(require='sandbox') produced TWO rows, the tenant resolution plus the sandbox_connect delegate; arm 2 a direct connect() produced a connect/live row with NO preceding tenant row -- THE BYPASS SIGNATURE, which is precisely _pg_probe_con's old shape and is what the source censuses cannot see; arm 3 the real probe recorded sandbox_connect/sandbox from _pycodemod_sql.py:860, where before the repair it would have recorded connect/live/rw. pycodemod --selftest 392/392 after wiring; --roundtrip 4/4 lossless. NOT YET DONE: no gate consumes lens_report(), and it is per-process by construction, so a runner must read it per-process rather than aggregate.}
}

@misc{sql-F33-probe-swallow-made-the-skip-unfalsifiable,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The probe's bare except made no-store and connected produce the same reading, so the selftest SKIP could not name its cause and every verdict about the probe was unfalsifiable.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=live-store-in-precommit. The witness. Both probe helpers ended in except Exception returning None, discarding the cause at the catch, so the dialect case could have been silently not-running for an unknown period while the suite reported green. This is sql-F4 and gate-F1 in one call. What it was hiding today is nothing, the sandbox is reachable and the case runs, but that was establishable only by positive control, since 392/392 with no SKIP line is equally consistent with the case having vanished. Repaired by capturing the exception into _PG_SANDBOX_ERR and adding pg_probe_status, which returns the reason and forces the attempt so unreachable cannot be read off a connection nobody opened. The catch stays non-fatal on purpose, since a census must degrade to pattern-only with no store. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): THE EXCEPTION-SWALLOW WAS THE SECOND HALF OF gate-F29 AND IS A SEPARATE DEFECT FROM THE TENANT. Both probe helpers ended 'except Exception: _PG_X = None', so a machine with NO STORE and a machine that CONNECTED produced the same reading from outside: None. The selftest's own SKIP line was honest about the epistemics ('UNMEASURED, not passed') and still could not say WHY, because the cause had been discarded at the catch. That makes every verdict about this probe unfalsifiable -- sql-F4's a-zero-needs-a-positive-control and gate-F1's vacuous-green in one call, and it is WHY the dialect case could have been silently not-running for an unknown period while the suite reported green. WHAT IT WAS HIDING, concretely: nothing today -- the sandbox is reachable and the case RUNS (verified by positive control, not by absence of a SKIP line, since 392/392 with no SKIP is consistent with both 'ran' and 'the case vanished'). The defect is that this could not have been established from the outside before the repair. REPAIR: _PG_SANDBOX_ERR captures 'Type: message' at the catch, and a new pg_probe_status() returns (con, reason) -- '' when connected, the exception otherwise, 'not attempted' if never tried. It FORCES the attempt, so a caller cannot read 'unreachable' off a connection nobody opened. The selftest SKIP now names its cause. The catch remains NON-FATAL on purpose: a census must degrade to pattern-only on a machine with no store, and gate-F24 is about opt-out from OBSERVATION, not about making an instrument fatal. Witness: pg_probe_status() -> (con, '') with current_database()='substrate_sandbox'.}
}

@misc{sql-F38-check-fresh-over-unexecutable-builders,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {query_defs --check reports 94 builders fresh while three of them are refused by the dialect at execution; freshness is a compile-and-cache-currency statistic and structurally cannot see semantic validity},
  check    = {refuses:python3,jea/metalanguage/query_defs.py,--explain,extract,--live},
  note     = {kind=finding by=witness-adequacy-write. Ⓐ5 ADEQUACY-GAP, adjudicated as ONE finding. Measured LIVE 2026-08-24: extract raises GroupingError (column nd.op must appear in the GROUP BY clause), reuse_rows_copy raises GroupingError on pt.text, clusters_overlap raises AmbiguousParameter (inconsistent types deduced for the sz parameter, double precision versus text). TWO distinct defect kinds, so this is not one typo propagated. POSITIVE CONTROL: --explain fanin --live exits 0 with a full plan over the same relations (_node, path_text, terms, node_child), so the three failures are properties of those builders and not of the mode, the tenant, or the schema. ⚑ AND MY FIRST READING WAS WRONG: I inferred from the compiled SQL that these reference an unmigrated catalog tier (sql-F26/F27/F29) and would fail on missing relations. They do not -- the relations resolve and the failures are genuine SQL semantics. The general form is a named verifier whose green does not entail the claim a reader takes from it, which is the same shape as the cap-set-not-enforced witness (swarm-F12) and the --tenants adjudication printing a clause with every conjunct false. Witnessed by refuses: on --explain extract --live, so it flips to OPEN when the builder is repaired -- the correct direction for a live defect. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Ⓐ5 ADEQUACY-GAP, and it IS one finding. query_defs --check exits 0 reporting 'query_sql_postgres.py fresh (94 builders, postgres)' while THREE of those 94 are refused by the dialect at execution. MEASURED LIVE 2026-08-24: extract raises GroupingError (column nd.op must appear in the GROUP BY clause), reuse_rows_copy raises GroupingError on pt.text, clusters_overlap raises AmbiguousParameter (inconsistent types deduced for the sz parameter, double precision versus text). Two distinct defect kinds, so this is not one typo. POSITIVE CONTROL: --explain fanin --live exits 0 with a full plan over the SAME relations (_node, path_text, terms, node_child), so the three failures are properties of those builders and not of the mode, the tenant, or the schema. THE GENERAL FORM: freshness is a compile-and-cache-currency statistic; validity is a dialect statistic; --check's green does not entail the second and a reader takes it as though it does. This witness holds while extract is still unexecutable and flips to OPEN when the builder is repaired, which is the correct direction: the finding is a live defect, not a standing fact.}
}

@misc{sql-F9-cancel-refusal-still-installed,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The sql-F9 repair is an installed refusal, so its witness must hold on non-zero; the standing witness it carried could not have noticed the guard being reverted},
  check    = {refuses:python3,jea/metalanguage/schema.py,--cancel,99999999},
  note     = {kind=finding by=witness-adequacy-write. Conversion of sql-F9 from standing to refuses:. DISCRIMINATION ESTABLISHED 2026-08-24 by running the command: python3 jea/metalanguage/schema.py --cancel 99999999 exits 2 with 'REFUSED - pg_cancel_backend(99999999) mutates the live store and no mutation intent was stated'. PID 99999999 is deliberately out of range so the probe can never cancel a real backend. If the mutation-intent branch is removed the command reaches pg_cancel_backend on a nonexistent PID, which postgres answers cleanly, so the witness flips to OPEN. ⚑ NOT VERIFIED BY REVERT: I did not remove the branch to observe the zero, because reverting a live-store guard to test a witness is not an acceptable experiment. The discrimination argument rests on reading the branch and on the refusal message naming the exact guard. ⚑ REJECTED ALTERNATIVE: schema.py --selftest (221/221) would NOT discriminate -- the repair is a branch in main() and no unit case covers it, so the suite passes with or without the guard. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): The sql-F9 repair is a REFUSAL and this witness holds only while it refuses. schema.py --cancel with a bare PID and no --apply/--dry-run must exit non-zero. Established as discriminating 2026-08-24 by running it: exit 2, message 'REFUSED - pg_cancel_backend(99999999) mutates the live store and no mutation intent was stated'. If the mutation-intent branch is reverted the command reaches pg_cancel_backend on a nonexistent PID and postgres returns cleanly, so the witness flips to OPEN. PID 99999999 is deliberately out of range so the command can never actually cancel anything. NOT VERIFIED BY REVERT: I did not remove the branch to observe the zero; the discrimination argument rests on reading the branch and on the refusal message naming the exact guard.}
}

