@misc{sql-F34-sqlite-reroute-landed-and-three-builders-remain,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The sqlite reroute landed but exposed two further fault classes visible only by running the repaired tools, and three compiled builders carry dialect-permissive SQL that postgres rejects},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {MERGED (operator 2026-09-25, one home in sql-oracle.bib). ⚑ PRIMARY WITNESS, merged verbatim from the gate-oracle.bib copy (kind=finding by=selftest-standardize-r6), whose claim read: sppf_query.py and reuse_tui.py are rerouted off catalog.db onto store.tenant, and 3 of sppf_query's 6 modes now work against live while 3 fail on sqlite-permissive GROUP BY in query_decl builders that query_defs --check reports fresh. Its note: Witness — pycodemod --importers sqlite3 no longer lists either file. sppf_query fanin 5 --live returns fan-in 87359, and sql 'select count(*) from _node' returns 443169 matching schema.py --census. extract/reuse/clusters raise psycopg GroupingError on nd.op/pt.text/v.name, reproduced independently via query_defs.py --explain extract --live. Four QB.run(...).fetchone() cursor-idiom sites repaired because query_builders.run returns a list, never a cursor. ⚑ THIS BIB'S ORIGINAL NOTE, verbatim: kind=finding by=witness-adequacy-write, PAIRING ONLY. Ⓐ10 bib-roster-drift: this witness was registered by a sibling without a bib entry, so it read as a roster/record disagreement in --list. The note is the witness's own, not re-measured here; see findings.py for the full text. SUMMARY: sppf_query.py and reuse_tui.py now resolve through store.tenant(mode=ro) instead of opening catalog/catalog.db by path, and both are absent from pycodemod --importers sqlite3. Two further fault classes surfaced only by RUNNING the repaired tools: a cursor-idiom fault at four call sites (QB.run(...).fetchone() against a run() that returns a list of name-keyed rows), and dialect-permissive SQL in three compiled builders (extract, reuse_rows_copy, clusters_overlap) that sqlite accepts and postgres rejects. ⚑ THIS ENTRY ALSO RECORDS A DUPLICATION I CAUSED: sql-F38-check-fresh-over-unexecutable-builders, filed by me in the prior round as the Ⓐ5 adequacy-gap, is ALREADY the closing paragraph of THIS finding -- 'query_defs --check IS GREEN while three of those 94 cannot execute'. I rediscovered it independently and by a different route (running --explain on each of the three) rather than finding it, because the witness had no bib entry and my reuse-search read the bib. An unpaired witness is not merely untidy bookkeeping: it is invisible to the search that prevents rework. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Ⓥ16 ⟡sqlite-reroute APPLIED 2026-08-24. scripts/sppf_query.py and scripts/reuse_tui.py no longer open catalog/catalog.db by path; both resolve through store.tenant(mode='ro'), argv-driven (--sandbox|--live), and both are now absent from pycodemod --importers sqlite3 (14 sites in 13 files, neither of them). THE REROUTE WAS NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT, AND THE BRIEF'S 'small, clean edit' UNDERSTATED IT BY TWO FAULT CLASSES. Beyond the dialect fault the brief named, TWO MORE were only visible by RUNNING the repaired tools -- the round-5 lesson again (a fact about the artifact, reachable only by opening it). (1) CURSOR-IDIOM: four call sites did QB.run(...).fetchone(), but query_builders.run NEVER returns a cursor -- it normalises to a LIST of name-keyed rows via _rows_of, precisely because a psycopg dict_row cursor unpacks to COLUMN NAMES (its own comment records 18,334 rows in, 1 dict entry out, silent through two full ingests). Three sites in sppf_query (_uid, cmd_support, cmd_clusters) + one in reuse_tui (core_count) -- all repaired, _one() is the single spelling. (2) DIALECT-PERMISSIVE SQL IN THE BUILDERS THEMSELVES, WHICH IS NOT MINE AND IS NOT REPAIRED: three compiled builders carry bare non-aggregated columns in a GROUP BY select -- legal in sqlite, rejected by postgres. extract -> GroupingError on nd.op; reuse_rows_copy -> on pt.text; clusters_overlap -> on v.name. All three live in jea/metalanguage/query_decl.py. CONFIRMED THROUGH A SECOND, INDEPENDENT TOOL: query_defs.py --explain extract --live raises the identical GroupingError, so the defect is in the builder, not in my rewiring. MEASURED AFTER: of sppf_query's 6 modes, fanin/support/sql WORK against live for the first time since the port (fanin 5 --live returns the real fan-in histogram topped by 87,359; sql 'select count(*) from _node' returns 443,169, matching schema.py --census exactly), and extract/reuse/clusters fail on the builder defect. ⚑ AND query_defs.py --check IS GREEN ('query_sql_postgres.py fresh, 94 builders, postgres') WHILE THREE OF THOSE 94 CANNOT EXECUTE AGAINST THE DIALECT THEY COMPILED FOR -- census-green-is-a-fact-about-the-census in its exact recorded shape: the freshness gate compares the compiled artifact to the builder source and never asks the engine whether the SQL runs.}
}

@misc{sql-F35-three-name-collision-is-same-relation-two-populations,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The three catalog name collisions are the same relations as the live event tier's, not homonyms, so the catalog tier should consume them; the real blocker is a split path_id key convention already live inside path_text},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {MERGED (operator 2026-09-25, one home in sql-oracle.bib). ⚑ PRIMARY WITNESS, merged verbatim from the gate-oracle.bib copy (kind=finding by=selftest-standardize-r6), whose claim read: terms/path_seg/path_text are the SAME relations in the catalog schema and the live event tier, not homonyms, so the catalog tier should consume them — but live path_id already carries TWO incompatible key conventions and that, not the name or the view/table type conflict, is what blocks both option (a) and option (b). Its note: Witness — terms — catalog declares term_id IS base64(text) and live satisfies it bit-for-bit, 'select term_id, encode(convert_to(text,UTF8),base64) from terms' returns the columns equal. path_seg — identical columns (path_id, ord, seg_term_id) and the SAME index name ix_pathseg_uniq, reported OK by schema.py --check-indexes, and reuse_catalog's own comment records the merge as already done under the tag catalog-pathseg-unique. path_text — the catalog VIEW's SELECT list equals the live TABLE's columns and the table holds the view's extension, so it is a materialization, the cheapest of the three, not a type conflict. THE SPLIT POPULATION — live path_text has 22374 rows of which only 594 satisfy path_id = base64(text), while 21574 sit at a fixed 11 characters, a 64-bit surrogate hash that is not reversible and not the declared contract. path_seg carries the same split at 121838 of 126190. A catalog tier joining on path_id would silently match 2.7 percent and return a plausible answer. Renaming cannot fix this because the names are correctly shared — only deciding the key function can. NOT DECIDED HERE. ⚑ THIS BIB'S ORIGINAL NOTE, verbatim: kind=finding by=witness-adequacy-write, PAIRING ONLY. Ⓐ10 bib-roster-drift: registered by a sibling with no bib entry. The note is the witness's own, not re-measured here; see findings.py for the full text and its three mechanical witnesses. SUMMARY: terms, path_seg and path_text in reuse_catalog._DB_SCHEMA are the SAME relations as live, established by (1) the content-address contract holding bit-for-bit (term_id equals base64 of text on every row sampled), (2) the same unique index name ix_pathseg_uniq on the same columns, with the catalog's own comment recording the merge as already having happened, and (3) path_text being a VIEW whose SELECT list matches the live TABLE's columns -- a view and a materialization of that view are one relation at two costs. ⚑ THE FINDING IS NOT THE COLLISION: live path_text holds 22,374 rows under TWO INCOMPATIBLE path_id conventions -- 594 content-addressed (path_id = base64(text)) and 21,574 at a fixed 11 characters, a 64-bit surrogate that is neither reversible nor the declared contract. path_seg carries the same split at 121,838 of 126,190. Two writers agreed on the SCHEMA and diverged on the KEY FUNCTION, so a catalog tier joining on path_id would silently match only the 594-row population and return a plausible 2.7 percent answer. Renaming cannot fix it; only deciding the key function can. Not decided there and not this agent's to decide. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Ⓥ8 THREE-NAME COLLISION ADJUDICATED: terms, path_seg and path_text in reuse_catalog._DB_SCHEMA are THE SAME RELATIONS as the live event tier's, not homonyms -- so the catalog tier should CONSUME them, never redeclare them. And the brief's sharpest framing (path_text is a TABLE live but a VIEW in the catalog, 'a type conflict, not just a name conflict') IS TRUE BUT IS NOT AN OBSTRUCTION: it is the SAME RELATION MATERIALIZED. WITNESS, three independent and each mechanical. (1) terms: catalog declares 'CREATE TABLE terms (term_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY, text TEXT)' with the ⟡content-addressed-interner contract stated in its own comment -- 'term_id IS base64(text), a bijective content-address, so interning is idempotent and shared by construction'. Live terms is term_id/text, and the contract HOLDS BIT-FOR-BIT: 'select term_id, encode(convert_to(text,UTF8),base64) from terms' returns the two columns EQUAL on every row sampled (U3Vic3RyYXRl = base64('Substrate')). Same columns, same key, same content-address function. Not a homonym. (2) path_seg: catalog declares (path_id TEXT, ord INT, seg_term_id TEXT) plus 'CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ix_pathseg_uniq ON path_seg(path_id, ord)'. Live path_seg is path_id/ord/seg_term_id and schema.py --check-indexes reports ix_pathseg_uniq OK -- the SAME INDEX NAME on the same columns. The catalog's own comment already records the merge as having HAPPENED: '⟡catalog-pathseg-unique: hoisted here from sppf_db, which kept a defensive IF-NOT-EXISTS copy'. Two writers were ALREADY declared to share this relation by design. (3) path_text: catalog defines it as a VIEW reconstructing the dotted path by GROUP_CONCAT over path_seg ordered by ord; live path_text is a TABLE of exactly (path_id, text) holding that same reconstruction (path_id 's0Epn+EzMwI' -> 'Substrate.Algebra.CayleyDickson.Hilbert2D.s'). The view's SELECT list and the table's columns are identical, and the table's contents are the view's extension. A view and a materialization of that view are the same relation at two costs -- which is why this is the CHEAPEST of the three to resolve, not the hardest. ⚑ THE REAL FINDING IS NOT A COLLISION AT ALL, IT IS A SPLIT POPULATION ALREADY LIVE INSIDE path_id, AND NOBODY HAS NAMED IT. Live path_text holds 22,374 rows under TWO INCOMPATIBLE path_id CONVENTIONS: 594 rows where path_id = base64(text) exactly (the catalog's declared content-address, length varying 44-76 with the path), and 21,574 rows at a FIXED 11 characters (= base64 of 8 bytes = a 64-bit surrogate hash, which is NOT reversible and NOT the declared contract). path_seg carries the same split, 121,838 of 126,190 surrogate. So the two writers agreed on the SCHEMA and diverged on the KEY FUNCTION, and the relation cannot say which convention a given path_id follows except by re-deriving base64(text) and testing. THIS IS THE THING THAT BLOCKS BOTH (a) BUILDERS AND (b) MATERIALIZE, and it is upstream of the view-policy question the operator has not ruled on: a catalog tier joining on path_id would silently match only the 594-row population and return a plausible 2.7% answer. Renaming would NOT fix it (the names are correctly shared); only deciding the key function does. ⚑ NOT DECIDED HERE AND NOT MINE TO DECIDE -- reported as the adjudication asked for, with the option space untouched.}
}

@misc{sql-F36-batch-half-was-hostage-to-tui-dependency,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The batch half of reuse_tui was hostage to the interactive half's dependency, so the mode the reroute was supposed to repair was unexercisable and could have been declared landed on a source read alone},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=witness-adequacy-write, PAIRING ONLY. Ⓐ10 bib-roster-drift: registered by a sibling with no bib entry. ⚑ THE NOTE IN findings.py IS DAMAGED and this entry does not restore it: several spans were eaten by bash command substitution when the witness was registered, which is why sql-F37 exists to supersede it. Read sql-F37 for the intact account; this entry exists so the key is paired rather than reading as a roster/record disagreement. SUMMARY as far as the surviving text supports: reuse_tui.py imported textual at MODULE scope, so the batch half -- which opens the store, derives orbit rows and writes a JSON cache, touching no widget -- died with ModuleNotFoundError before argparse ran on a machine without textual. REPAIR: the import is guarded and the failure DEFERRED rather than swallowed, so only run_tui refuses, naming the cause and the batch alternatives. Two further sites fell out of actually RUNNING it, neither visible by reading: argparse refused --live/--sandbox because store.tenant reads sys.argv directly while argparse validates it (now generated from store.TENANT_FLAGS so the roster cannot drift from the resolver's, per gate-F19), and the cache's provenance field was the constant catalog.db, already wrong before the reroute and wrong in a new way after, now recording the TENANT. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): REPAIRED IN PASSING, AND IT IS THE REASON Ⓥ16 COULD NOT HAVE BEEN VERIFIED WITHOUT RUNNING IT. reuse_tui.py imported at MODULE scope, so — which opens the store, derives orbit rows and writes a JSON cache, touching no widget — died with ModuleNotFoundError before argparse ran, on a machine where textual is not installed (this one). The batch half of the tool was hostage to the interactive half's dependency, and the mode I was sent to repair was therefore UNEXERCISABLE: a reroute could have been declared landed on a read of the source while remaining unrunnable. REPAIR: the textual import is guarded, App falls back to so the class body still evaluates, and run_tui — the ONE entry point that needs widgets — exits with the cause and the batch alternatives. The failure is DEFERRED, not swallowed (): launching the browser without textual still refuses and says why. TWO MORE SITES FELL OUT OF ACTUALLY RUNNING IT, neither visible by reading. (1) argparse REFUSED --live/--sandbox, since store.tenant reads sys.argv directly while argparse validates it — the flags are now declared, GENERATED FROM store.TENANT_FLAGS rather than spelled by hand so the roster cannot drift from the resolver's (gate-F19's two-halves-that-do-not-flip-together). (2) The cache's provenance field was the CONSTANT "catalog.db" — already wrong before the reroute, and wrong in a NEW way after it. It now records the TENANT (live/sandbox), which is the fact that actually distinguishes two real stores whose wrong-tenant read is silent (). MEASURED WORKING: → '15289 units, 75 shared subtrees' and '1884 defCopy instances from the live store', so has_copy resolved through schema.columns_of too. Tenant refusal fires on with no flag. still runs with NO tenant, correctly, because it reads only the cache and opens nothing.}
}

