}

@misc{sql-F27-catalog-tier-is-blocked-by-view-policy,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The catalog tier cannot be declared as handed, because four of its relations are convenience VIEWS the schema declaration is built to refuse and two base tables collide with an existing tier.},
  check    = {standing},
  note     = {kind=finding by=sql-oracle via substrate. Witness registered in findings.py, kind standing. A prior round's verdict — the repair is declaring the tier in schema_decl — was a tool-capability guess asserted without reading schema_decl, and MEASURING IT INVERTS IT. ⚑ The four relations catalog_query names are all VIEWS in reuse_catalog's schema, not base tables, and schema_decl cannot emit convenience views BY CONSTRUCTION: view-purge deleted VIEWS, _TIER_VIEWS and view_ddl and dropped sqlalchemy-views from pyproject, keeping the reason verbatim — a convenience view is a second spelling of the query algebra, and it is NOT behind a flag because a retained emit is the thing a future reader turns back on instead of composing. ⚑⚑ view_ddl returned for SHADOWS only, under an admission criterion the file states: a view is admissible iff it is the reconstruction claim of a relation SUPPOSED to be reconstructible, one per tier, no others. SHADOWS holds exactly one entry. So declaring the four as views is the exception that swallows the rule. ⚑ THE OWED WORK IS NOT WHAT WAS HANDED OVER: the nine base tables are admissible and unblocked, but terms and path_seg ALREADY EXIST in the event tier, so the tier as written collides on two relations — a schema question nobody has adjudicated. The four views must become named query builders in the qinfra layer instead, which is a larger and differently-shaped job with a prerequisite decision. ⚑⚑ The policy is the operator's own, quoted in the file. The case stays RED regardless: no relation was declared. ⚑ WITNESS NOTE, moved verbatim from findings.py WITNESSES (2026-09-26): Round 4's verdict — 'the repair is declaring the tier in schema_decl.py' — is a class-(A) tool-capability-guess (meta-F3): a claim about schema_decl's behaviour asserted without reading schema_decl. MEASURED, and it inverts. The four relations catalog_query names (structs, edges, in_degree, module_edges) are ALL VIEWS in reuse_catalog._DB_SCHEMA, not base tables: structs is a 13-line JOIN over _structs/path_text/terms with two correlated GROUP_CONCAT subselects computing fp and unhold_fp; edges is a JOIN of _edges to path_text twice; in_degree and module_edges are views over views. And schema_decl CANNOT emit convenience views by construction. ⟡view-purge (2026-08-12) deleted VIEWS, _TIER_VIEWS, view_ddl and _view_ddl_named and dropped sqlalchemy_views from pyproject; the residue is kept verbatim at schema_decl.py:1308-1333 with the reason: a convenience view is 'a second spelling of the query algebra', and NOT BEHIND A FLAG because 'a retained emit — even unused, even opt-in — is the thing a future reader turns back on instead of composing'. view_ddl came back 2026-08-13 for SHADOWS ONLY, under a WRITEABLE admission criterion the file states explicitly (schema_decl.py:1224): 'a view is admissible here IFF it is the reconstruction claim of a relation that is SUPPOSED to be reconstructible — one per such tier, no others'. SHADOWS is the whole population and holds exactly one entry (core_decoded_shadow); _tier emits nothing else. The four catalog relations are conveniences, so declaring them as views is the exception that swallows the rule the pairing exists to make visible. THE POLICY IS THE USER'S OWN, quoted in the file: 'I know I have a hard policy against CREATE VIEW'. SO THE OWED WORK IS NOT WHAT I WAS HANDED. Declaring the NINE BASE TABLES (terms, path_seg, _structs, _members, _member_heads, _refs, _edges, _module_edges, _modules, meta) as a tier is admissible and unblocked — but terms and path_seg ALREADY EXIST in the event tier, so the catalog tier as written would COLLIDE on two relations, which is a schema question nobody has adjudicated. The four VIEW relations must instead become named query builders in the ⟡qinfra layer (where a parameter can be pushed in, which is precisely what the purge's objection demands), and catalog_query's consumers rewritten onto them. That is a larger and differently-shaped job than 'declare a tier', and it has a prerequisite decision — the terms/path_seg tier collision — that must be settled first. ⚑ THE CASE MUST STAY RED regardless: no relation was declared this round.}
}

@misc{sql-F28-sqlite-import-census-by-importers-not-literal,
  section  = {finding},
  claim    = {The sqlite census is one number covering three unrelated verdicts, and the instrument that produces it must be --importers rather than --literal or --calls.},
  check    = {standing},
